    "q-179429": {
      "dom_id": "step-q179429",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are two events such that $P(A)=\\frac{7}{15},$ $P(B)=\\frac{9}{15},$ and $P(A\\cap B)=\\frac{3}{15},$ find $P(A\\cup B).$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284903/assets/35e807b7de787f50d0cbff770e4083cef84b4d35030b4c506b1db5646cf7a358.png)\n\nThe addition law for probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B).\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate $P(A\\cup B),$ we apply the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{9}{15}-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Venn diagram corresponding to this situation is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14284903/assets/78456e7cfa16752d8dc4a8ee10b46f9e06e5cd734f0fda94abdeb9f1276e2538.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
