    "q-120558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q120558",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "John selected a pair of socks out of his drawer without looking in the drawer. He has $5$ pairs of black dress socks, $4$ pairs of brown dress socks, $10$ pairs of black sports socks, and $3$ pairs of white sport socks. What is the probability of selecting a pair of black socks or a pair of sports socks?",
      "worked_solution": "Let us call $B$ the event \"a pair of black socks has been selected,\" and $S$ the event \"a pair of sports socks has been selected.\" Then the probability of selecting a pair of black socks or a pair of sports socks is $P(B\\cup S),$ and we can apply the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B\\cup S)=P(B)+P(S)-P(B\\cap S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $P(B),$ $P(S),$ and $P(B\\cap S).$\n\n- There are $5+10=15$ pairs of black socks among the $5+4+10+3=22$ pairs of socks, so we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{15}{22}.\n$$\n\n- There are $10+3=13$ pairs of sports socks among the $22$ pairs of socks, so we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=\\frac{13}{22}.\n$$\n\n- There are $10$ pairs of black sports socks. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap S)=\\frac{10}{22}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B\\cup S) & =P(B)+P(S)-P(B\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{22}+\\frac{13}{22}-\\frac{10}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
