    "q-87367": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87367",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A fair spinner with $15$ equal sectors is labeled with the numbers $1$ through $15.$ What is the probability of getting a prime number or an even number after launching the spinner?",
      "worked_solution": "Let us call $R$ the event \"we get a prime number,\" and $E$ the event \"we get an even number.\" Then the probability of getting a prime number or an even number is $P(R\\cup E),$ and we can apply the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $P(R),$ $P(E),$ and $P(R\\cap E).$\n\n- There are $6$ prime numbers among the $15$ labels of the spinner ($2,3,5,7,11,13$), so we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{6}{15}.\n$$\n\n- There are $7$ even numbers among the $15$ labels of the spinner ($2,4,6,8,10,12,14$), so we have\n\n$$\nP(E)=\\frac{7}{15}.\n$$\n\n- There is only a single number, $2,$ that is both prime and even. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap E)=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15}+\\frac{7}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
