    "q-1829": {
      "dom_id": "step-q1829",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A card is drawn at random from a standard $52$-card deck. What is the probability of drawing a card labeled with a number less than $4$ or a spade card?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits. Number cards are the cards labeled $2$ through $10$ in each suit.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $A$ the event \"a card labeled with a number less than $4$ is selected\" and $B$ the event \"a spade card is selected.\" \n\nThe required probability is $P(A\\cup B),$ which we can find using the addition law\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B).\n$$\n\nLet's find $P(A)$ and $P(B).$\n\n- There are $2\\cdot 4=8$ cards labeled with a number less than $4$ in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{8}{52}=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- There are $13$ spade cards in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- There are $2$ spade cards labeled with a number less than $4$ (the $2$ of spades and the $3$ of spades). Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=\\frac{2}{52}=\\frac{1}{26}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of the known information into the addition law, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{52}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
